## Actual custody seam and compatible state map

Current direct install creates an operator key/signature and immediately calls found without keeping PlannedInstall before the request. It saves final config after acceptance. Therefore the new explicit direct-cohort install must not merely swap NEW_REGISTER for @6 in that old short path. Reuse the existing plan preparation/persistence and installPlanned execution for the selected cohort, preserving its complete plan and attempt before any request. The --plan path prints the derived register for actual deployment registerScope preparation and sends nothing. --planned sends only the original retained founding and selected pin.

Current planInstall and plain install deliberately allow replacing an unattempted plan; install.scope.test.ts contains a real witness that an unsent plan followed by plain install founds another register. Both commands already block an attempted plan. The new selector must not erase that legacy witness or silently widen it into a new-cohort downgrade. Concrete safe extension: protect any new explicit selection against an existing incompatible pin, and protect every existing Counting@6 plan (even unattempted) against the no-option/default path. A compatible new-cohort --plan returns the retained plan/seed instead of signing again; compatible direct or --planned execution uses that retained plan. Legacy --planned remains exact for every supported old pin.

The existing definitely-unsent legacy no-option replacement case can remain unchanged within the old/default cohort, while no explicit cohort switch or Counting→default downgrade is permitted. If owner interpretation prohibits all preexisting unattempted-plan replacement, that is a concrete compatibility change to this existing native witness and must be recorded with the identity owner before editing it; it must not be hidden as a parser adjustment. This map does not implement or advertise a plan-discard/abort/recreate protocol. Attempted/unknown plans always restore/reconcile the original envelope, service, selected pin, derived scope and deployment binding. An expired never-sent original may be refused; expiration never licenses changing an unknown request.

